---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import { DEMO_TERRAIN_CONFIG } from '../../lib/isomap/terrain';
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Map renderer preview',
    description: 'The same isometric map renderer as the homepage, driven by arbitrary input data.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    The map below runs on the exact same 3D engine as the homepage (<code>mountIsomapScene</code>{' '}
    in <code>src/lib/isomap/scene.ts</code>) — the only difference is the <strong>input</strong>:
    instead of the homepage's four guilds, it's given two smaller, neighboring territories. Neither
    the renderer nor the terrain generator (<code>generateTerrain</code> in{' '}
    <code>src/lib/isomap/terrain.ts</code>) hardcode any names or coordinates — the whole layout
    (name, grid position, size) is just data, so any other set of territories renders the same way.
  </p>

  <div id="demo-map-root" class="demo-map-root"></div>

  <p>The input used above (<code>DEMO_TERRAIN_CONFIG</code>, exported from <code>isomap/terrain.ts</code>):</p>

  <pre><code>{JSON.stringify(DEMO_TERRAIN_CONFIG, null, 2)}</code></pre>

  <p>
    That input can come from anywhere — e.g. a real guild list pulled from a server — so the same
    component can later visualize actual territories, or other plugin features, not just the
    static example on this page.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
  .demo-map-root {
    position: relative;
    height: 420px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--fg-hairline);
    background: #4f8a41;
  }
  .demo-map-root :global(canvas) {
    display: block;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { mountIsomapScene } from '../../lib/isomap/scene';
  import { DEMO_TERRAIN_CONFIG } from '../../lib/isomap/terrain';

  const root = document.getElementById('demo-map-root');
  if (root) mountIsomapScene(root, DEMO_TERRAIN_CONFIG, { frustumHalfHeight: 20 });
</script>
